361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (In UWOC, information is transmitted as binary bits \(0 and 1\) using optical signals. As light travels through water, turbidity \(NTU\) causes absorption and scattering, which changes the received optical signal amplitude. These variations make fixed-threshold detection unreliable and can lead to bit errors. To overcome this challenge, supervised machine learning models are used to classify transmitted bits based on received signal values and turbidity information, enabling more robust communication under varying underwater conditions.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
